        "content": [
          {
            "type": "thinking",
            "thinking": "Roster country lists Yazaki and Sumitomo as JPN, Aptiv as USA — but that's corporate HQ, not origin for duty purposes. Actual duty treatment depends on where the wire harness was manufactured or substantially transformed, and these are commonly produced in Mexico, the Philippines, or Morocco regardless of parent company nationality.",
            "signature": ""
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "**Roster countries:** Yazaki — JPN; Sumitomo Electric — JPN; Aptiv — USA.\n\n**Same treatment on this basis:** Yazaki and Sumitomo Electric share a roster country (JPN), so they'd be grouped together; Aptiv (USA) would not.\n\n**What the roster country does not tell you:** US import duty turns on the country of origin of the goods — where the harness was manufactured or substantially transformed — not on the supplier's roster/corporate country. The roster field says nothing about plant locations, and wire harnesses are labour-intensive and commonly assembled in third countries (Mexico, the Philippines, Morocco, Eastern Europe). It also tells you nothing about tariff classification, preference eligibility (e.g. USMCA content rules), or whether a given bid would be filled from a US plant or an offshore one. Aptiv's USA roster entry does not imply domestic production, and the two JPN entries do not imply Japanese production.\n\nTo settle the duty question you'd need per-bid manufacturing origin data, which this roster does not contain."
          }
        ],
